package codegen

// Package codegen is the contract between the compiler's front end and
// its code generation backend, a port of the Go compiler's package
// codegen (backend.go): the backends the driver may name, the
// optimization levels a build may run at, and Refuse, which says whether
// a program may be lowered at all; and of the C backend, Go's
// codegen/c, kept in this package while it is the one backend: the
// header, tack.h (header.tck, Go's header.go), with the names it may not
// give (reserved.tck, Go's reserved.go and any.go's anyIDs and
// viewAnyID), and the lowering of a program to one C translation unit,
// ported in stages, of which the core, E1, is here.
//
// generate refuses what Go's Refuse refuses, then rejects, with
// header_diags, a program whose types, fields, results structs or
// extern funcs tack.h could not name, as Go's generate does before it
// lowers anything, and then lowers the program (lower, gen.tck), as Go's
// generate does: the globals noted, the string pool filled, every
// declaration lowered before anything is written, and then the file in
// C's order of declaration, the preamble, the types, the pooled strings
// and the builtins' prototypes, the globals, the results structs, the
// prototypes, the functions and C's main. header renders tack.h as Go's
// Header does, which the driver's --port-header writes with nothing
// lowered and --emit-header writes, once the program is lowered, into
// the package directories, as Go's writeHeaders does.
//
// # The files
//
// Each file ports a part of Go's codegen/c, its header naming which:
//
//   - gen.tck: the generator's state and its top level, Go's gen.go from
//     generate to emitEntryPoint, with the globals, the constant pool,
//     the results structs, the prototypes, the signatures and a
//     function's body;
//   - pool.tck: the string pool, Go's collectStrings and its helpers,
//     and string.go;
//   - unused.tck: what no other function names, Go's unused.go and
//     unusedParams;
//   - walks.tck: the walks of the program and of a body the core asks,
//     usesType, narrowsFloats, the divisions, mutated and caseVarsRead;
//   - types.tck: the walk of the program's types, Go's emitTypes;
//   - stmt.tck: the statements, from generateBlock to generateVar, with
//     defer.go's funcFrame, generateJump and stops;
//   - expr.tck: the expressions, a machine of routines (see below);
//   - order.tck: the order of evaluation, Go's order.go but its spills;
//   - names.tck: the C names and literals, mangling among them, and
//     names.go.
//
// # The stages
//
// The backend is ported in stages, as tackc's porttest stages its cases
// by what Go's backend runs on them: E1, the core, here; E2, values,
// structs, arrays, unions, switches, slices, views, any, swap, division
// and shifts, several values and the checked forms; E3, owned values and
// containers, lists, maps, arenas, pools, defers, ?, drop, replace, the
// free helpers and regions; and E4, generics and functions, instances,
// closures, function values, fields loops and make of Data. The core
// asks of every name, call and type whether a later stage's feature is
// there, and ports what Go does when it is not; where Go would go on to
// lower the feature, the port answers not_ported, which the driver
// reports as not implemented, exit status 3. Everything is lowered into
// buffers before anything is written, as Go's generate does, so a
// program declined at any point writes nothing: no partial C, and no
// number used by a lowering cut short shows anywhere. The places the
// core declines are each named in a comment, with the stage whose work
// it is. A function Go's later stages own but every core program runs,
// a predicate whose answer is no, is ported here as that answer: the
// type checker's frees of an own parameter, a list's builtins, a scope
// parameter, an any parameter, a variadic, a view argument and a
// generic callee among them. Two of Go's walks the port does not make,
// since only a later stage acts on what they find: the guard of the
// owned values a ? may need to free, guardOwned, which holds nothing
// but around a ?, E3's, and the switch on a union's payloads bound in
// place, inPlace, which only a payload or an owned element sets.
//
// # Numbering
//
// C names carry numbers Go hands out as it lowers: temporaries, tack_
// and a prefix and the count of temporaries named so far in the file,
// and pooled strings, tack_str and their place in the pool. Every
// temporary is named where Go names it, in Go's order, the ones a render
// later thrown away named among them: a spill scope rendered again, deep
// (see expr.tck), keeps the counter where the first render left it, as
// Go's errRetry does, so the numbers it used stay used. The pool fills
// in the order of Go's walk; Go finds a string in it by scanning, and
// here a map finds it, which gives the same label. The type table adds
// no type while the core lowers: only a binding does, E4's, and
// arena.go's list of an arena's elements and list.go's views, E3's.
//
// # Stack discipline
//
// Go renders an expression by recursion, a frame a level, and a chain
// of operators, of && among them, is as long as its source makes it, so
// the expressions are rendered by a machine with a stack of its own
// (expr.tck): each of Go's functions on the way, expr, exprSteps,
// spilled, exprNode, binaryExpr, otherScope, unaryExpr, callValue,
// callExpr and the rest, is a routine of it, whose steps do what Go's
// function does between two of its calls, in Go's order. Every walk of
// the tree keeps a stack of its own (see push_children in pool.tck).
// Statements nest in blocks, and blocks in statements, as deeply as the
// parser lets them, a few frames a level, each kept small; a chain of
// else ifs and a run of statements are walked in a loop, so neither
// costs a frame a link. Go's commaList recurses as deep as the
// logarithm of the number of steps it joins, and so does its port.
//
// # Lines
//
// A lowered body is a list of Lines, Go's []string: each a run of one
// buffer, Gen.text, that every line is written to, or one of the two
// markers Go's indent puts around a nested block, which emit_func turns
// into indentation as it writes the lines, as Go's emitFunc does, so a
// line is never rebuilt for each level around it. What noteNames records
// Go records of every identifier of every body; only names that start
// tack_ are ever asked about, so only those are recorded here.
//
// It takes a checked program as the type checker hands it over, a
// typeck.Typed (see package typeck): the program, its files, its type
// table and every annotation Go's checker writes on the tree that Go's
// backend reads. The type table comes as a types.Bound, which answers
// every query under the binding in force, as Go's Table does, and hides
// the Table's own queries, which answer as if nothing were bound: the
// backend binds a generic function's type parameters while it lowers an
// instance, and C names carry the IDs of the types a binding adds, so
// no query it makes may skip the binding, and through a Bound none can.
//
// Go registers its backends from init into a map, which Tack has no
// place for: there is one backend, c, so the registry is the list
// backend_names, in Go's sorted order, which has_backend searches, as
// Go's Get does, and which --backends writes, one a line, as Go's Names
// gives them. Go's Register, which panics on a name registered twice,
// has nothing to check here. A Typed exists only for a program the type
// checker has checked, so of Go's Refuse's refusals only the one for a
// program with errors can be made here; the others, for no program and
// for one never checked, cannot happen.
//
// Where Go's backend reports an internal error whose message names a Go
// type with %T, an unknown declaration, statement or expression, the
// port declines instead. A call that passes a testing.Site argument, a
// struct literal, is E2's, and declined where its strings are pooled.
//
// The commands a build runs, Go's CompileCmd, CompileStdCmd, BuildCmd
// and VersionCmd, come with the driver's builds, which the port does not
// do yet.
